course: everyday
lesson: 107
title: Oven, hob, kettle, toaster and dishwasher
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Kitchen machines are helpful, but they hold heat, steam, gas and electricity that can hurt you if you are careless.
explain: A hot surface does not always look hot: a dark glass hob or a pan can burn you minutes after it is switched off.
explain: Little children are at most risk, because they reach up and grab without knowing what is hot.
explain: For example, a boy in Vancouver making tea keeps the kettle cord tucked away, because a dangling cord could pull a full boiling kettle onto someone.
fact: An open oven door and the inside of the oven are hot enough to burn skin instantly.
fact: Keep small children away from an open oven and close the door quickly.
fact: If a gas flame goes out while the gas is on, turn the knob off and tell a grown-up; unburnt gas can fill the room and explode.
fact: A dark glass hob does not show its heat and can stay hot after it is switched off.
fact: Unplug a kettle before filling it, fill it only to the line and keep its cord away from the edge.
fact: Keep hot drinks out of reach of toddlers; a cup of tea can scald a small child even twenty minutes later, because it stays hot a long time and their skin is thin.
fact: Never carry a hot drink while holding a little child.
fact: If toast starts smoking, press the cancel lever and unplug the toaster.
fact: Old crumbs in a toaster can catch fire, so a grown-up empties the crumb tray when it is unplugged and cold.
fact: Put knives and forks in the dishwasher points down, or lay knives flat in the top.
fact: Wait a few minutes and stand back when opening a dishwasher that has just finished, because hot steam can scald.
fact: Close an open dishwasher door, because people trip over it and can fall onto the knives.
exercise: The oven door is open and your little sister toddles near. What do you do? | Keep her away and close the door quickly | Let her look inside the hot oven so she can learn | Tell her to touch it gently | Leave it open to let the heat out
exercise: Why is an open oven dangerous for a small child? | The door and inside can burn skin instantly | The light inside can hurt her eyes | The oven door is too heavy to lift | The smell of the cooking inside can make a small child ill
exercise: The flame on the gas hob has gone out but the gas is still on. What do you do? | Turn the knob off and tell a grown-up | Light it again yourself with a match from the drawer | Leave it on for the grown-up to find | Open the oven to check it too
exercise: Why is unburnt gas dangerous? | It fills the room and can explode | It makes the kitchen too cold | It turns the water in the pipes off | It only smells bad and nothing else
exercise: The black glass hob looks dark and cool. Should you touch it? | No, it can stay hot after it is switched off | Yes, dark means it is cold | Yes, if it was switched off a second ago it is cool already | Yes, glass never gets hot
exercise: You are filling the kettle for tea. What is safe? | Unplug it, fill to the line and tuck the cord away | Fill it right to the very top while it is still switched on | Leave the cord hanging over the edge | Fill it under the tap while it is plugged in
exercise: A toddler is near the counter while tea is made. What is right? | Keep hot drinks out of reach | Let the toddler hold the cup if it is cooling | Put the tea on the edge where you can see it | Carry the toddler and the tea together
exercise: Why can a cup of tea scald a small child even after twenty minutes? | It stays hot for a long time and their skin is thin | Tea gets hotter as it stands | Only cold tea is dangerous for children, never hot tea | Tea always boils again by itself
exercise: Your toast starts to smoke. What do you do? | Press the cancel lever and unplug it | Wait to see if it gets better | Poke the toast out with a fork while it is still on | Pour water into the toaster
exercise: Why should the crumb tray of a toaster be emptied? | Old crumbs can catch fire | Crumbs make the toast taste bad | Crumbs stop the toaster switching on | Crumbs attract only flies
exercise: How do you put knives into the dishwasher basket? | Points down, or flat in the top | Points up so they get clean | Any way, as they will be washed | Wrapped in a tea towel
exercise: The dishwasher has just finished. How do you open it? | Wait a few minutes, open slowly and stand back | Open it fast and put your face right in to check the plates | Open it at once and grab the plates | Open it while it is still washing
exercise: The dishwasher door is down and you are walking past. What do you do? | Close it | Step over it each time | Leave it for someone else to close | Sit on it to rest
exercise: Why is an open dishwasher door a danger? | People trip over it and can fall onto knives | It lets the clean dishes get dirty again straight away | It uses more water when it is open | It makes the kitchen too hot
test: Oscar in Manchester smells gas and sees the hob flame has gone out. What should he not do? | Try to light it again himself | Turn the gas knob off | Tell a grown-up straight away | Keep away from the hob
test: Which is true about a dark glass hob? | It can be hot even when it looks dark | It is never hot after a minute, even straight after cooking | It always glows red when it is hot | It cannot burn anyone
test: A kettle cord hangs over the edge of the counter. What could happen? | A child pulls it and boiling water falls on them | The kettle boils more slowly | The cord gets cold and breaks | The kettle switches itself off when its cord is hanging down
test: Why should you never carry a hot drink while holding a baby? | The drink could spill and scald the baby | The baby will want to drink the tea | Carrying both at the same time is too heavy to manage | Babies do not like the smell of tea
test: A toaster is full of crumbs. When is it safe for a grown-up to empty the tray? | When it is unplugged and cold | While it is toasting | As soon as the toast pops up | When it is still warm and plugged in
test: Aria unloads the dishwasher and a knife is point up. What could have happened? | She could have been stabbed reaching in | The knife would wash less clean | The knife would fall through the basket | The dishwasher would not start
test: Why does steam from a dishwasher hurt more than warm air? | Steam is very hot and scalds your skin | Steam has soap in it that stings your skin | Steam is colder but wetter | Steam has germs in it
test: Your mum is taking a tray out of the oven and your little brother runs in. What is the most helpful thing? | Keep him back away from the oven | Ask him to help carry the tray | Open the oven door wider | Tell him to touch the door gently
test: toast's smokin!! what do i do | Press the cancel lever and unplug the toaster | Dig the toast out with a fork, it's quickest | Throw a cup of water on it | Wait a bit, it might stop
test: mum the hob looks off now, can i lean on it | No, a dark glass hob can still be hot | Yes, if it isn't glowing red then it's cool | Yes, glass hobs never get hot | Only if you lean with your sleeve
test: gonna fill the kettle, its still plugged in tho | Unplug it first, fill to the line, tuck the cord away | Fill it to the very top so it lasts | Fill it where it sits, plugged in is fine | Leave the cord hanging down so it's easy to reach next time
test: dishwasher just beeped, grab the plates now? | Wait a few minutes and stand back as you open it | Open it fast and look right inside | Pull the plates out at once while hot | Open it straight away and put your face in the steam to check
explain: A family in Ohio left a pan of oil on the hob and went to answer the door; it caught fire, and when someone threw water on it a ball of flame burst up and burnt the kitchen.
explain: A boy in Leeds poked a knife into a toaster to get bread out; he got an electric shock and was lucky not to be badly hurt.
fact: Water on a burning oil pan makes a fireball; you turn off the heat and cover it with a lid or fire blanket if an adult can do it safely, or get out and call for help.
fact: Putting metal into a toaster while it is plugged in can give you an electric shock.
test: pan's on fire!! get water? | No, water makes a fireball; get out, shout for an adult, call the emergency number | Yes, throw a big jug of cold water on it as fast as you can, it puts fires out | Carry the burning pan to the sink and run the tap over it | Blow on it hard
test: toast stuck, gonna fish it out with a fork | Unplug it first and let it cool, metal can shock you | Use a fork while it's on | Use a knife, it's longer | Shake the toaster upside down while it's still on
test: kettle cord hangin off the counter, lil sis below | Push it back, she could pull boiling water over herself | Leave it, she can't reach that high anyway, she's too little | Tie a knot in it | Only move it at night
test: did u hear Ollie stuck a knife in the toaster to get his bread out n got electrocuted n his hair stood up like a cartoon lol. what's the real lesson | Never put metal in a plugged-in toaster; unplug it first | Toasters are only dangerous when they are cold | Use a fork instead, since forks are safe in any toaster | Shake the toaster upside down while it is on
test: my nan says her friend's house blew up cos the gas went out on the cooker n they lit a match, apparently the whole block went. what's the true part | Unburnt gas can explode, so turn it off and tell a grown-up | Gas cookers always blow up the whole block | Gas is completely harmless once the flame on the cooker has gone out | Lighting a match is the way to check for gas
test: Grace's mum threw water on a chip pan n the flames went up to the ceiling, Grace told me not to tell anyone. should i spread it | No, she asked you not to; the lesson is no water on oil fires | Yes, because it is too exciting to keep quiet | Yes, but tell only the teachers and a few of your closest friends | Yes, so her mum learns her lesson in public
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a dark glass hob presents a particular hazard. | It does not display its heat and may remain hot after switching off | It heats only when touched by a person's hand | It is cooled instantly when it is switched off | It conducts electricity to any person who touches it with a wet hand
exam: Whereas the flame of a gas cooker is extinguished while the gas remains on, determine the appropriate action. | Turn off the knob and inform an adult | Relight it at once with a match | Leave it on so that the gas may escape out of the window | Open the oven door to warm the room
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning hot drinks and toddlers. | A cup of tea may scald a small child twenty minutes later | Tea is harmless to toddlers once some cold milk has been added to it | Hot drinks scald only during the first minute | A small child cannot reach a cup on any table
exam: Pursuant to the smoking of toast within a toaster, ascertain the correct procedure. | Press the cancel lever and unplug the toaster | Remove the toast with a knife at once | Pour water into the toaster to cool it | Shake the toaster while it is still plugged in
